# Vercel Web Analytics Installation Report

## Summary
Successfully installed and configured Vercel Web Analytics for this Next.js project using the App Router architecture.

## Changes Made

### 1. Package Installation
- Installed `@vercel/analytics` package (version included in package.json)
- Updated package.json and package-lock.json accordingly

### 2. Code Changes
**Modified: `app/layout.tsx`**
- Added import: `import { Analytics as VercelAnalytics } from "@vercel/analytics/next";`
- Added `<VercelAnalytics />` component at the end of the `<body>` tag
- Used alias `VercelAnalytics` to avoid naming conflict with existing Firebase Analytics component

## Implementation Details

The project already had a custom Firebase Analytics implementation, so I:
1. Imported the Vercel Analytics component with an alias to avoid naming conflicts
2. Placed the `<VercelAnalytics />` component after the existing Firebase Analytics setup
3. Both analytics solutions now coexist and will track page views independently

According to the official Vercel documentation (fetched from https://vercel.com/docs/analytics/quickstart), the Analytics component for Next.js App Router should be placed in the root layout file, which is exactly what was implemented.

## Verification

✅ Build completed successfully with no errors
✅ TypeScript compilation passed
✅ All changes staged with git

## Next Steps

After deployment to Vercel:
1. Enable Web Analytics in the Vercel dashboard (Analytics section → Enable button)
2. Verify analytics tracking by checking browser Network tab for requests to `/<unique-path>/view`
3. Monitor analytics data in the Vercel dashboard

## Files Modified
- `app/layout.tsx` - Added Vercel Analytics component
- `package.json` - Added @vercel/analytics dependency
- `package-lock.json` - Updated with new package dependencies
